-- Transcription of layout-language-consult.md §5.4 (lines 545-583),
-- clean-room LengYue, landscape class. Disclosed adjustments:
--   - no-op root sizing supplied (document elides it, same as all five
--     encodings — see q5go.lyt's comment for the full reasoning).
--   - `T(...)[BLACK BOX]{pref 1fr, min envelope over tabs}`: the bare
--     "min envelope over tabs" prose isn't a literal value, so 'min' is
--     omitted here and the compiler derives the T-node's structural
--     minimum as the componentwise max of its children (§4.1 line
--     298-302).
--   - the five bare `CP-library, CP-cards, CP-settings, CP-analysis,
--     CP-other` names inside T(...) (line 576) aren't bracketed
--     `widgetid[domain,...]` leaves at all — they're prose naming the
--     control-panel tabs, which §2 explicitly commissions as an opaque
--     black box outside the A/go|common|debug|board classification. We
--     give each one domain='blackbox' (the sixth domain literal added in
--     lyt_ast.py specifically for this region) and no facets.
--   - `A_go[go, action]{28px}`, `A_common[common, action]{28px}`: the
--     bare-fixed-extent shorthand (min=pref=max=28px).
--   - `I_engine[...]{28px, envelope: {...}}`: fixed 28px reservation with
--     an attached (documentation-only, for this 1-D sizing model) content
--     state list; states renamed to valid identifiers
--     (`connected_5digit_latency` for the document's
--     "connected×latency≤5digits", since '×' and '≤' aren't legal
--     identifier characters).
--   - F4 DISCLOSURE (review row 1609,
--     .claude/dispatch-reports/lyt-compiler-prototype-review.md finding
--     F4 item 4): every one of the T(...)'s children below is given
--     its own `min WRAPPER_MIN` (300px). §5.4's own text (line 577) gives
--     no such per-child min — the bare "min envelope over tabs" phrase is
--     prose, not a value, and the compiler derives the T-node's OWN
--     structural min as the componentwise max of its children's declared
--     mins (§4.1 line 298-302). Left with no per-child min at all, that
--     derivation would yield T's own min = 0px, which reads as "the
--     black-box region gets no floor at all" — an equally unfaithful
--     reading of the same prose. We import WRAPPER_MIN onto every child
--     instead, so the derived T floor is a real, if approximate, 300px —
--     the SAME choice lengyue_portrait.lyt already makes (with 200px,
--     disclosed there).
--   - AMENDMENT 3 (ledger row 1715, gap-amendment-build): three `gap`
--     declarations, all mapped onto `frontend/src/assets/css/theme.css`'s
--     `--space-*` tier scale (that file's own §"Four tiers for gap,
--     padding, margin", read in full):
--       - the OUTER H(...) (boardRail vs. board area vs. the side control
--         column) gets `gap 12px`, `--space-medium` ("section-level gap,
--         content-block separation") — for the split between the page's
--         MAJOR REGIONS. DISCLOSED DOWN-TIER FROM `--space-loose` (20px),
--         per the original build report's finding (900x600 regresses to
--         INFEASIBLE at the wider tier); unchanged by the additions
--         below.
--       - the side column's own V(...) (the go-actions/engine-info/
--         common-actions strip stack feeding the tree/panels row) keeps
--         `gap 4px`, `--space-tight` ("button-cluster gaps, icon
--         spacing, tight rows").
--       - NEW: the tree/panels/preview row (see the "TREE ALWAYS
--         VISIBLE" note below) is itself an H(...) needing its own gap
--         between tree, the control-panel tab group, and the preview
--         board — `gap 4px`, `--space-tight` again, matching the
--         side column's own tier (this row IS one of the side column's
--         "tight rows").
--
--   - TREE ALWAYS VISIBLE (ledger row ~1735, commissioner review of the
--     clean-room mockups: "'Tree & Panels' look like it has tabs, one
--     for tree and one for the other stuff in the control panel, but the
--     tree should always be visible"). The tree leaf is pulled OUT of
--     the T(...) exclusive group and made a permanent sibling of it,
--     inside a new H(...) row: `H(tree, T(CP-*), previewBoard)`. This
--     is NOT a new invention — it reproduces, almost exactly, the SHAPE
--     the as-is encoding already transcribes for the real app
--     (`fixtures/transcription/current_row_asis.lyt`'s own
--     `H(tree{140px,drag-persisted}, resizerInner, T(CP-*){WRAPPER_MIN})`
--     — this file omits the resizer leaf, since the census census
--     doesn't carry a dedicated resizer widget in this consolidated
--     tree, and drops `drag-persisted` since this prototype's L4
--     accounting is unimplemented (README.md's L4 section) and neither
--     clean-room encoding used it before). Tree's reservation, 140px
--     fixed (`min=pref=max=140px`), is grounded directly in
--     `frontend/src/state/layout-model.ts`'s `TREE_PANEL_MIN_WIDTH_PX`
--     AND `TREE_PANEL_DEFAULT_WIDTH_PX` (both 140, the row-axis policy
--     object every width class currently shares) — the "sensible
--     reservation" the commission asked to ground against the live app.
--     The T(...) node keeps `{pref 1fr}` (elastic, min still derived from
--     its now-five children's own WRAPPER_MIN, unchanged), and picks up
--     what's left of the row after tree, the tight gaps, and the new
--     preview board below claim their fixed shares. The T node's own
--     `[BLACK BOX]` tag is, if anything, MORE accurate now than before —
--     every one of its remaining children is domain='blackbox', where
--     previously the tag covered tree too (domain='board', never
--     actually blackbox).
--
--   - boardRail (ledger row 1735, commissioner ruling: "the clean-room
--     encodings OMITTED the boardRail... it returns as a
--     USER-TOGGLEABLE presence slot — user-initiated, space-releasing
--     ... wired into the corner presence-menu... DEFAULT OFF"). Identity
--     and ~168px reservation grounded in
--     `fixtures/transcription/current_row_asis.lyt`'s own transcription: that file's
--     `@toggle(user, release) {min 168px, pref 168px, max 168px} V(
--     loadSave, boardRail, addBoard, jankTest, preview )` is the real
--     app's sidebar rail. This clean-room file, being function-
--     consolidated (per the original commission), does not carry
--     loadSave/addBoard/jankTest/preview as separate leaves — those are
--     out of THIS commission's naming (only "boardRail (the open-boards
--     thumbnail rail)" was named), so only `boardRail` itself is added,
--     at the SAME 168px width the whole rail column occupied (the one
--     concrete number the ruling supplies: "reservation grounded in the
--     as-is encoding's value"). Placement: leftmost child of the root
--     H(...), i.e. "the left edge as today" — the ruling's own
--     conservative default, unchanged from the as-is app's real DOM
--     order (`App.vue`'s sidebar rail sits left of the main H, before
--     the toggle rail even). Presence: AMENDMENT 4 (ledger row 1737,
--     per-valuation presence solving, SPEC-AMENDMENTS.md) makes this
--     genuinely `@toggle(user, release)` in this file's own concrete
--     syntax — no longer only a generator-level (`emit_mockup.py`
--     `TOGGLE_TARGETS`) affordance layered on top of an otherwise
--     `@fixed` AST. The DEFAULT-OFF state itself still lives at the
--     registration layer (`runner.py`'s `Registration.default_valuation`,
--     a Python-declared fact — same "no objectivesec/classsec concrete
--     syntax" precedent `lyt_ast.Program`'s own docstring already uses
--     for classes/objective), not in this file's text — a slot's
--     DECLARED presence type (this file) and which VALUATION solves it
--     absent (the registration) are two different facts, only the first
--     of which belongs in the encoding per this prototype's existing
--     division of labor. `emit_mockup.py`'s `TOGGLE_TARGETS` entry for
--     "Board Rail" is unchanged (still the UI-registry source of truth
--     for the CSS mockup's corner menu) but is now cross-checked by a
--     regression test against `runner.py`'s own declared default
--     valuation, so the two can no longer silently drift apart.
--
--   - previewBoard (ledger row 1735, SECOND commissioner amendment: "a
--     variation-preview mini board (lizzieyzy / chess-preview paradigm),
--     LOWER RIGHT of the layout... aspect-1 leaf... modest reservation,
--     grounded against the existing library MiniBoard's 160x160
--     precedent... user-toggleable... DEFAULT OFF"). Placement: the
--     rightmost slot of the new tree/panels row (`H(tree, T(CP-*),
--     previewBoard)`) — that row is the bottom-most element of the side
--     column, which is itself the page's rightmost region in landscape,
--     so its own rightmost slot IS the page's lower-right corner
--     (already home to the corner presence-menu button per the prior
--     build report's own WITNESSED claim #4). Reservation: 160px fixed
--     on BOTH axes (`min=pref=max=160px, aspect 1`), the exact square
--     grounded in `frontend/src/components/library/LibraryPreviewPane.
--     vue`'s own "160×160 box (both dimensions capped, not just width)"
--     precedent (that file's own comment, read in full). This is the
--     SECOND aspect-locked leaf in this program (the board `B` is the
--     first) — `compiler.py`'s aspect-slack machinery
--     (`_collect_aspect_slack_terms`, Stage 1.5) is written generically
--     over every aspect leaf in the tree, not hardcoded to one, so no
--     compiler change was needed; `emit_mockup.py`'s own board-only
--     `is_board_leaf` cross-axis-containment check was generalized to
--     `is_aspect_leaf` (any leaf with a declared `aspect`, not just
--     widget id `"B"`) for the same reason on the generator side — see
--     that module's own docstring for the disclosure. Presence:
--     also AMENDMENT 4 (see boardRail's own header note above for the
--     full rationale) -- genuinely `@toggle(user, release)` in this
--     file's own concrete syntax now, DEFAULT-OFF state still declared
--     at the registration layer (`runner.py`).
--
--     DESIGN NOTE (Vue-realization phase, no build action taken here):
--     the commissioner separately floated boardRail-as-POPOVER (overlay
--     stratum, zero standing cost, triggered from chrome) as an
--     alternative to the presence-slot shape built here. Recorded as an
--     open fork for whoever picks up the eventual Vue implementation —
--     this commission was explicitly to keep building the presence-slot
--     version.
--
--   - REPAIR PASS (ledger row 1781, W1 REPAIR; review finding A,
--     `.claude/dispatch-reports/lyt-w1-skeleton-review.md` §2): the side
--     column's own `min 340px` and A_go/I_engine/A_common's `28px` each
--     (84px total) are BOTH raised here — a disclosed, MEASURED
--     reservation-correction, not a re-derivation from the original
--     §5.4 prose (which never anticipated three encoding-level bands
--     being realized as ONE merged Toolbar.vue mount, roadmap §8 W1's
--     own judgment call). The rejected prior W1 attempt's realization
--     mounted the merged Toolbar unchanged into the original 340×84
--     reservation; measured (Playwright, headless chromium, the actual
--     merged Toolbar content: title + engine-metrics + slider/PBO/setup
--     popovers + move-nav + the 8-button engine-controls cluster)
--     against a WIDTH SWEEP of the realized `.lyt-toolbar-strip` box:
--     340px -> 533px tall, 400px -> 405px, 480px -> 341px, 560px ->
--     253px, 640px -> 238px, 720px -> 178px, 820px (the column's own
--     max) -> 178px (no further gain past ~720px — the content's own
--     wrapped-cluster floor). 340px is proven-infeasible without either
--     scrolling (rejected: "never hide content" is the standing law) or
--     silently clipping (Finding A itself).
--
--     The width chosen is 480px, NOT the originally-tried 640px: 640px
--     regresses `test_lengyue_landscape_default_valuation_solves_optimal`'s
--     1366x768 case from OPTIMAL to INFEASIBLE (verified — `pytest
--     tests/test_lyt.py -k test_lengyue_landscape_default_valuation_
--     solves_optimal`), because it pushes the side column's structural
--     min past what the CP-SAT solve can still satisfy alongside the
--     board's own `pref maximize` claim at that size. 480px is the
--     largest of the swept widths that keeps every size the base test
--     suite already pins OPTIMAL (1920x1080/2560x1440/3440x1440/1366x768)
--     genuinely OPTIMAL (verified the same way) — a real, disclosed
--     ceiling this repair pass respects rather than overrides. The
--     tradeoff is a taller-than-ideal toolbar reservation (341px
--     measured floor at 480px, vs. 178px at 720px+) in exchange for not
--     touching the base suite's existing feasibility pins.
--
--     84px -> 384px (128px x 3, keeping the three-leaf structure the
--     roadmap's own A_go/I_engine/A_common merge note already commits
--     to displaying as one spanning mount) covers the measured 341px
--     synthetic-sweep floor at 480px, revised up once more (122px x 3 =
--     366px) after the REAL running app (every component actually
--     mounted, not the width-forced sweep harness) measured 374px at
--     1280x1024/1024x700 -- 8px over the 366px reservation. 128px x 3 =
--     384px leaves a genuine ~10px margin over the real-app floor. This
--     is a REAL, disclosed regression in available board
--     width/height at the narrowest viewports the mandate tests
--     (1024x700, 1280x1024) relative to the original 340px/84px
--     transcription — flagged for commissioner ratification in the
--     repair build report as the single biggest open concern; a
--     from-scratch CP-SAT re-solve incorporating the merged-mount
--     reality as a modeled constraint (rather than this measured,
--     hand-set correction bounded by NOT regressing the existing
--     feasibility pins) is the more rigorous fix a follow-up wave
--     should pursue if the commissioner wants a tighter number than
--     this repair pass's conservative one — or wants to spend the
--     1366x768 OPTIMAL pin to buy back a narrower/shorter toolbar
--     reservation, a tradeoff this repair pass declined to make
--     unilaterally.
--
--   - W4 FLOOR SOFTENING (Q3 ruling, ledger row 1848; commission
--     `.claude/dispatch-reports/lyt-vue-realization-roadmap.md` item 6):
--     "NO compact class" was the standing ruling -- the SAME tree must
--     become solver-FEASIBLE at 1280x1024/1024x700/900x600 by lowering
--     MINS (never gaps -- Amendment 3's own "rhythm is not negotiable
--     under board-maximization" rationale is respected verbatim here;
--     no `gap` value in this file changed), with the board-first
--     objective governing which floor gives way. Three mins lowered,
--     all on the SIDE COLUMN / control-panel side of the tree (never
--     the board composite itself, which stays untouched per that same
--     objective):
--       - side column's own outer `min`: 480px -> 280px. DISCLOSED,
--         NOT independently re-measured against a live Playwright
--         width-sweep the way the REPAIR PASS's own 480px number was
--         (see that section above for the methodology) -- item 2's own
--         toolbar-structure rework (envelope-reserved metric cells,
--         a real SLIDERS button, one home for Load/Save SGF) tightens
--         the toolbar's realistic width need somewhat, and the
--         toolbar's own proven flex-wrap mechanism (W1 REPAIR, this
--         file's own header above) degrades ANY width squeeze onto
--         more rows rather than clipping -- so a narrower floor is
--         functionally safe, just not empirically re-swept at 280px
--         specifically. A follow-up wave re-running the REPAIR PASS's
--         own measured-sweep methodology at this narrower floor is the
--         more rigorous confirmation, named here as the honest gap
--         this pass leaves (same disclosure posture the REPAIR PASS
--         section above models).
--       - the five CP-* children's own per-child `min`: was the
--         `WRAPPER_MIN` sentinel (300px, `loader.WRAPPER_MIN_PX`,
--         SHARED with `current_row_repaired.lyt`/`current_row_asis.lyt`
--         -- two unrelated historical/comparison fixtures outside this
--         item's scope). Rather than lower the SHARED global constant
--         (which would silently move those two fixtures' own
--         feasibility pins too, a cross-boundary side effect this item
--         doesn't authorize), this file now declares its own literal
--         `160px` per child instead of the sentinel -- scoping the
--         floor-softening to exactly this encoding, none other. The
--         T-node's own derived min (componentwise max of its children)
--         drops from 300px to 160px accordingly.
--       - tree leaf: `140px` (min=pref=max, fixed) -> `110px`. Verified
--         DEAD for live rendering purposes before lowering it: W3's own
--         resizer machinery (`useResizablePanel.ts`'s
--         `effectiveTreePanelWidthPx`) UNCONDITIONALLY overrides this
--         leaf's compiled track at Vue-runtime
--         (`App.vue`'s own `lytTrackStyleOverrides`, LytNode.vue's own
--         "Resizer drag overrides" header note: "wins VERBATIM over
--         that child's own compiled track shape") -- so this number is
--         consulted ONLY by the offline CP-SAT solver's own feasibility
--         check in this prototype's test suite, never by the live app.
--         Lowering it is therefore a zero-live-behavior-change lever,
--         used purely to buy solver headroom.
--
--     Net effect (verified by re-running `tests/test_lyt.py` after this
--     change): the three previously-`INFEASIBLE` landscape sizes
--     (1280x1024, 1024x700, 900x600), both under the "all-present" and
--     "default" presence valuations, flip to `OPTIMAL` -- the pinned
--     `known_infeasible_by_valuation` set in
--     `test_generated_pages_embed_valid_overlay_json_matching_overlay_sizes`
--     is updated accordingly (that test's own docstring is updated in
--     the same change). Every size that solved `OPTIMAL` before this
--     change (1920x1080/2560x1440/3440x1440/1366x768/1080x1920-in-
--     landscape-stays-infeasible-for-an-unrelated-reason) still does --
--     lowering a `min` only relaxes a constraint, it can never turn a
--     previously-feasible point infeasible.
--
--   - W4 FLOOR CORRECTION (independent review,
--     `.claude/dispatch-reports/lyt-w4-chrome-review.md` item 6): the
--     280px side-column floor above was disclosed as "functionally
--     safe, just not empirically re-swept" -- the re-sweep this repair
--     pass performs shows that disclosure was wrong. A live measured
--     width-sweep of the real running app (forcing `.lyt-node`'s
--     side-column track through descending widths and walking every
--     descendant for right-edge overflow) found `.setup-toolkit`
--     (`SetupToolPalette.vue`'s permanently in-flow reservation, per
--     that component's own no-occlusion standing law -- the palette is
--     `visibility: hidden` when closed, never `display: none`, so its
--     296px measured width is a flex-column sibling minimum at ALL
--     times, not just while open) clips starting at 330px and reaches
--     52px of horizontal overflow at the shipped 280px floor. The
--     toolbar's flex-wrap degradation (the mechanism the disclosure
--     leaned on) cannot rescue this: wrapping moves OTHER row content
--     onto new rows, but does not touch `.setup-toolkit`'s own minimum
--     content width. Measured true floor: 0px overflow at 335px, first
--     overflow (2px) at 330px. Corrected floor: 345px -- the measured
--     335px floor plus a ~10px margin, the same posture the REPAIR
--     PASS section above used (374px measured -> 384px shipped, "10px
--     margin over the real-app floor"). Verified (re-running
--     `tests/test_lyt.py` after this correction): all three sizes this
--     item flipped to OPTIMAL (1280x1024, 1024x700, 900x600) remain
--     OPTIMAL at 345px -- the floor-softening's net effect above is
--     preserved, not undone; 345px was not swept per-pixel against the
--     live app the way 335px/280px were (that would require re-running
--     the review's own Playwright width-sweep harness, not this
--     encoding's own solver), so a follow-up wave re-confirming 345px
--     against the live DOM the way this correction re-confirmed 335px
--     is the honest remaining gap, named per this file's own established
--     disclosure posture.
--
--   - LYT TOOLBAR ONTOLOGY REENCODE (commissioner-ratified 2026-08-11,
--     ledger rows 1930/1931; `.claude/dispatch-reports/
--     lyt-toolbar-ontology-reencode.md`). Four ratified points, this
--     file's own share of the delivery:
--       (1) BOARD CONTROLS GO TO THE BOARD -- unaffected here (the
--         move-navigation cluster relocates into the Vue realization's
--         StatusBar.vue, mounted at this file's own I_board/A_board
--         leaves; no `.lyt` structural change, since those leaves'
--         reservation was already large enough -- see the dispatch
--         report's own measurement table).
--       (2)+(3) ONE ENGINE CLUSTER (envelope-reserved) / ONE APP CLUSTER:
--         the three-leaf `A_go`/`I_engine`/`A_common` strip (28px x 3,
--         later 128px x 3 under the W1 REPAIR/W4 passes above) is
--         RETIRED. Two leaves replace it: `A_engine[go, action+info]`
--         (connect/disconnect + the engine-controls button cluster +
--         engine metrics, `envelope: {disconnected, connected}` --
--         genuinely reserved at the MAX across states now, closing the
--         commissioner's witnessed defect "actions still reorganize the
--         buttons...e.g. connecting") and `A_app[common, action]`
--         (Load/Save SGF + sliders/setup/PBO popovers + the engine URI
--         editor + the locale picker). This SHIFTS the tree/panels/
--         preview row's own tree path from '2.3' to '2.2' (one fewer
--         V-child ahead of it) -- every path-keyed consumer across the
--         Python tooling and the Vue realization was updated in the
--         same change; see the dispatch report's "path census" section.
--       (4) RE-MEASURE, RE-SOLVE: `60px`/`160px` (envelope-reserved /
--         fixed respectively) are a REAL Playwright measurement of the
--         realized `ToolbarEngineCluster.vue`/`ToolbarAppCluster.vue`
--         components (both engine states) at the column's own real-app
--         OPERATING width (~400px -- the REPAIR PASS's own real-app
--         measurement precedent, not the absolute 345px floor, which is
--         reached only under extreme squeeze), plus a ~10px margin, the
--         SAME posture the REPAIR PASS and W4 FLOOR CORRECTION sections
--         above use. DISCLOSED TENSION, named rather than hidden: at the
--         absolute 345px floor specifically, the connected-state engine
--         cluster (measured 78px) and the app cluster (measured 199px)
--         both exceed this reservation (60px/160px) -- `.lyt-toolbar-
--         strip`'s own `overflow-y: auto` degrades this to an internal
--         scrollbar (content stays reachable, never clipped/hidden) at
--         that one narrow edge case rather than an outright layout
--         break. A more conservative alternative (88px/210px, derived
--         from the 345px-floor measurement itself) would close this gap
--         entirely at the cost of ~78px more standing side-column height
--         at every OTHER width -- named here as the follow-up a future
--         wave can take if the commissioner wants zero-scroll at the
--         hard floor over the current, narrower reservation. Net effect
--         on the total side-column reservation: 384px (128px x 3) ->
--         220px (60px + 160px), ~164px returned to the board at every
--         width where the column doesn't hit its own absolute floor --
--         see the dispatch report's measurement table for the full
--         before/after.
--   - OPTION C TAB-SKELETON ENCODING (2026-08-11, ledger row 1937's
--     ratified consult, `.claude/dispatch-reports/lyt-tab-region-
--     consult.md` §3-C/§6/§8/§9; work item lyt-tab-skeleton-encoding).
--     Solver-side only -- frontend/src and LytNode.vue are untouched;
--     `emit_layout_tree.py`'s plain-leaf-T assertion is retired (that
--     module's own docstring has the full disclosure) so this composite
--     interior still emits as ONE collapsed `blackbox` leaf, byte-identical
--     realization.
--
--     BLACKBOX RE-HOMING (§6.3): every CP-* leaf's domain moves from the
--     retired `blackbox` domain literal to its true domain (`common` for
--     library/cards/settings/other's leaves, `go` for the analysis tab's
--     leaves, `debug` for the Other tab's ColorDebugStrip) plus the new
--     `boundary` sizing-bag flag ("an unmodeled subtree stands here").
--     Geometry-inert re-homing on its own; genuinely new leaves (below)
--     carry real sizing.
--
--     CP-library / CP-cards stay opaque single leaves (no witnessed
--     symptom, ADR-0008: don't classify what hasn't crystallised) but gain
--     an honest classification: `content unbounded` (virtual-scrolled
--     browse tables), `scroll v` declared (§8.4's ratified table row) --
--     L5a's coverage requirement is satisfied by this leaf's own `scroll v`
--     (its root-to-leaf path's sole scroll owner, L5b).
--
--     CP-settings OPENED ONE LEVEL (item 4, §3-C/§8.2): `V(substrip,
--     pane)`. `settingsSubstrip`: `content bounded`, no scroll declared
--     (§8.4: settings sub-tab strip is bounded chrome, no-scroll). Its
--     28px height is the SAME fixed-strip convention this file already
--     uses for A_engine/A_app-adjacent rows. `settingsPane`: a SINGLE
--     opaque leaf standing for whichever of the six sub-tab bodies is
--     showing (`SettingsTab.vue`'s own `activeSubTab`) -- item 4's own
--     text commissions exactly one opaque pane leaf, not six; per §8.4's
--     table two of those six bodies (Advanced Registry, Keybindings) are
--     genuinely `unbounded`/scroll-declared while the other four are
--     bounded/designed/no-scroll, and a single leaf cannot carry two
--     different classifications for two different (mutually-exclusive)
--     runtime states the way the T's own sibling machinery does one level
--     up -- DISCLOSED CONSERVATIVE CHOICE: `settingsPane` is classified
--     `unbounded, scroll v` (the WORST-CASE superset of its six possible
--     bodies), which is honest (never claims a state that could actually
--     overflow undeclared) but over-reserves scroll-affordance for the
--     four bounded bodies. A future wave that opens CP-settings a SECOND
--     level (a T of six named panes, mirroring CP-analysis's own nested T)
--     would let each body carry its own true classification -- named here
--     as the residual this wave does not close, not silently absorbed.
--
--     `settingsSubstrip`'s WIDTH DEMAND -- the outer `V(substrip,pane)`
--     wrapper's own T-child `min 838px` (both axes; see the BOTH-AXES
--     TENSION note below) -- is a ch-measured envelope over the six
--     static sub-tab labels, GROUNDED: label text from
