--     FLOW-CAPABLE SETTINGS SUBSTRIP (2026-08-11, work item
--     lyt-settings-live-opening, ledger rows 2007/2009): the 838px
--     single-row settings-substrip demand this REPAIR note documents
--     above was, per row 2007's ruling, NEVER the only honest reading --
--     the substrip is WRAP-CAPABLE, and its declaration becomes an
--     ENVELOPE over PACKED ROW-STATES derived from the six label texts +
--     spacing tokens (width demand converts to modeled HEIGHT demand; no
--     scroll declaration -- the substrip stays `content bounded`, no
--     scroll, L5 coverage satisfied by the envelope itself). Mechanism
--     per row 2009: deterministic, order-preserving, greedy left-to-right
--     line fill ("vim-gqq-style"), implemented as the small typed seam
--     `research/lyt/flow.py` (`pack_rows`/`flow_envelope`/
--     `narrowest_width_for_row_count`; that module's own docstring names
--     it explicitly as the WORKED PROTOTYPE a future settings-pane flow
--     extension generalizes from). The compiler stays a VERIFIER, per the
--     ruling -- `flow.py` is an OFFLINE calculator this encoding's author
--     consults to pick a grounded design point, not a second optimizer
--     wired into `compiler.py`'s own CP-SAT model (packing-into-bins is
--     not the linear relation the solver reasons about).
--
--     THE DESIGN POINT, mechanically computed (not hand-picked): the SAME
--     six item widths the 838px-repair table above already grounds
--     (113/177/153/153/137/105px = ch*8 + 16px padding + 1px border,
--     summing to 838px for one row) are fed to
--     `narrowest_width_for_row_count(item_widths, target_rows=2,
--     search_ceiling=838)`, which returns **443px** -- the NARROWEST
--     width at which greedy packing needs at most 2 rows (row 1:
--     session+analysisEnv+cardSets = 443px; row 2: advancedRegistry+
--     analysis+keybindings = 395px). `flow_envelope` at that width, with
--     `row_height_px=28` (this file's own established fixed-strip
--     convention, unchanged) and `row_gap_px=4` (`--space-tight`, the
--     SAME tier every other tight inter-row gap in this file uses),
--     yields **height=60px** (2*28 + 1*4). Both numbers are asserted by
--     `research/lyt/tests/test_flow.py`'s own
--     `test_settings_substrip_design_point_is_443px_2row_60px` regression
--     -- a change to the label set or the TabWidget padding/border
--     constants that moves this number is a DELIBERATE encoding change,
--     caught, not silently drifting.
--
--     WHY 2 ROWS, NOT 1 OR 3+: 1 row (838px) is the INFEASIBLE reading
--     this whole work item exists to retire (838px exceeds the side
--     column's own separately-ratified `max 340px+60ch` = 820px cap,
--     REGARDLESS of screen size -- REPAIR's own "18px over the cap"
--     finding). 3 rows (306px) would be a smaller, ALSO-feasible width
--     floor but a taller (92px) and visually cramped strip for no
--     additional feasibility benefit over 2 rows -- 443px already clears
--     the 820px cap with ~377px of margin, comfortably inside the side
--     column's OWN pre-existing width budget at every pinned OPTIMAL
--     size this file's own suite checks. 2 rows is the design point that
--     buys feasibility at the SMALLEST height cost.
--
--     WHAT CHANGES, mechanically: `settingsSubstrip`'s own declared
--     sizing moves from `{28px, boundary, content bounded}` (implicitly
--     one-row) to `{60px, boundary, content bounded}` (the flow-derived
--     2-row height, still a FIXED reservation -- `content bounded`
--     stays honest; flow's own row-count is a DESIGN decision baked into
--     one number here, not a runtime-varying quantity this 1-D-per-slot
--     language could express as "N rows at width W" for a range of W
--     anyway, per the BOTH-AXES TENSION note above and SPEC.md §4.2's own
--     "envelope states are documentation, not a computed sizing input"
--     disclosure -- L3's envelope machinery is NOT reused here, since
--     L3's envelope is about CONTENT STATES of one reservation, not
--     ROW-PACKING states, and conflating the two would be the exact
--     category error ADR-0008/§13.2 already retired `blackbox` for). The
--     wrapping `V(settingsSubstrip, settingsPane)` composite's own
--     T-child `min` -- the number BOTH-AXES TENSION applies identically
--     to width and height -- drops from **838px to 443px**
--     (max(443 width-floor, 60+4+200=264 height-floor) = 443, the width
--     term now dominates, same as before but at the much smaller
--     flow-derived number instead of the single-row ch envelope).
--
--     FULL SOLVE, RETURNED TO FEASIBILITY: `research/lyt/tests/
--     test_lyt.py` and `test_emit_layout_tree.py`'s own re-run after this
--     change (delivery report has the literal pytest transcript) finds
